Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:10.730 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Music]  in the year 1822  in the little town of arbor france was  born a son to the village tanner  whose shop was right beside the river  the boy was named louis  louis pasteur he was a plotting careful  boy  with no particularly great talents but  he did have an indomitable will  a fiery temper and the great capacity  for work  he became a chemist  it was louis pasteur who opened up which  menace its very  existence he made us conscious for the  first time  of this vast and vital microscopic life  by his daring and original experiments  he the chemist made modern medical  science possible  by sweeping away the ancient theories of  disease  by such things as spirits dispositions  and devils they were devils  but of a very different kind concrete  living destructive microbes  he definitely and completely smashed the  belief  that microbes came into existence by  spontaneous generation  his opponents shouted and ranted against  him  but he performed his experiments and  silenced the unbelievers  with indisputable facts microbes  are born from other microbes they do not  come to life automatically and  spontaneously  from nowhere this  did pastor prove  the academy of medicine in paris was the  citadel of the medical profession in  france  among its members were the most  illustrious physicians and scientists  as well as the most stubborn  in the general auditorium of this famous  academy  it was the custom for the members to  present their theories and discoveries  to be discussed by the other colleagues  many times had pasteur faced the men of  this academy  and many times had the meetings turned  into stormy arguments  between pasteur the chemist and those  prejudiced and narrow-minded enough  to regard him as a quack  how often had he presented his  discoveries  only to shock the elders from their  complacency  how often had he roused them from their  small and unprogressive worlds  to vistas of that new universe of deadly  as well as beneficial microbes  at times to be applauded and at times to  be laughed at  despised and accused as a faker  before this assembly he had presented  his experiments in fermentations  thus saving the wine industry for france  and gave to the world  the process now known as pasteurization  a simple method of heating from this  platform had he explained  how he had saved the silk industry by  tracking down  the microbe and in this auditorium  had he given science his most famous  discovery  immunization by a process of building up  a resistance against a specific germ  first by a weak and then by increasingly  stronger injections of that very same  germ  he had discovered that he could create  immunity  the first step in curing many terrible  diseases  he had discovered the nature of vaccines  he would make his patient just a little  sick with a very weak  germ and permit him to recover then he  could withstand the most  deadly microbes of the same species  he had tamed microbes to fight other  microbes  the same one that killed was the one  that guarded against  death tirelessly he and his faithful  assistants  searched experimented and searched again  it needed the patience ability and  imagination  of a true scientist and genius and  pasteur was all of that  but these men only blustered their  objections  pasteur's weapons were his laboratory  and scientific truth  these stubborn enemies of his are now  long since forgotten  while louis pasteur will never be  when pasteur was nine years old he had  seen a peasant suffering from an attack  by a mad dog  he heard the groans of pain as the man  was tortured by the horrible disease  hydrophobia this memory no doubt  prompted pastor to find the cause of  that scourge of mankind  he drove his assistance almost to  desperation  but finally he knew he had succeeded  but the great proof still remained to be  shown  could pastor save human lives  dared he try his cure on the human body  he faced a terrible choice and in a  letter to a friend  he wrote he would inoculate himself with  a frightful germ  and then try to cure himself  but fate mercifully relieved him of such  a decision  a boy joseph meister was brought to him  he had come from a nearby village  the old grandfather told pastor how  little joseph had gone out to the fields  to play  only two days ago  he had often done so and never before  had there been any danger  happily scampering down a familiar  country road  which had never held any fears for him  he had suddenly met the strange and  raving mad dog  the beast ravaged by the terrible rabies  had attacked the boy and mangled him  with 14  wounds save my boy pleaded the old man  it is still not too late tenderly  pastor carried the boy to a bed was this  his opportunity to try his new vaccine  why hesitate and yet he was  experimenting with a human life  such a young life too should he or  should he not  the boy would surely die from the dread  disease  he might live if pasteur would try his  vaccine  it had worked on his animals he had  proved that beyond a doubt  there was no other protection against  this disease that strangled its victims  only pastor could save him maybe  his faithful assistant the physician who  could administer the cure did not  hesitate  there was no choice it was sure death  or possible life the vaccine was  prepared  for the final test  that night july 6 1885  the first inoculation against rabies was  made  then past anxious hours anxious days  each day joseph meister received an  increasingly stronger dose  of the life-saving vaccine building up  an immunity against the time  when the deadly germ should strike its  fatal blow  of what did pastor think as he paced the  room  perhaps he remembered his many failures  perhaps he remembered how his anthrax  cure for cattle  at first proved to be a huge success and  then had failed  that was four years before in 1881  when he had tried his first vaccine for  anthrax  would he fail now how often had he  fumbled his way  how often had he made mistakes he  couldn't be wrong now  he had been stubborn about hydrophobia  and had not stopped until he had been  absolutely sure  well as sure as he could be  on the 14th day the unseen enemy  was conquered  all the world then paid tribute to this  man of science  this benefactor of mankind institutes  were established in his name  streets were called after him statues  were built  louis pasteur's name became known to the  four corners of the earth he was the  first man to make the world fear and  respect  the almost invisible microbe more  dangerous than  canon were these tiny creatures and yet  more beneficial too than the greatest of  healers  pastors life had been a busy one he had  worked hard with his hand  and his brain and nature began to take  its toll  his hair turned white and age sat  heavily upon his shoulders  there was still much work to be done but  his family and his friends persuaded  him to rest let others take off the  tremendous tasks before mankind  where he had left off and so  he heeded their words and went to live  in his home in the country  there to think and even to plan greater  experiments against the invisible  enemies  those hordes of vicious microbes  even when he himself was inactive  physically  he was in constant touch with the work  in progress at the institute  named in his honor and his spirit was  tireless  then to crown a career as brilliant as  any man's in history  whether king or philosopher france rose  to pay a final tribute  to her native son a great meeting was  held in his honor  at the famous university of the sorbonne  in paris  it was in 1892 on pastor's 70th birthday  and this was a great day for france and  for louis  pasteur  men famous the world over were there to  pay him homage  men from every walk of life scientists  statesmen teachers poets  and students representatives from every  civilized nation on earth  were there to greet him and yet  among all those present there were none  so famous and so revered  as he himself he had shown mankind the  road to health  he had shown mankind the path to a  greater freedom and the more generous  life  he had opened another door of knowledge  and truth  and had shown the world a greater glory  in the protection  of the human body  leaning on the arm of the president of  the french republic  pasteur entered the great hall cheered  lustily by all present  his final words were to those young  students of the famous university  and he said this do not let yourselves  be  tainted by a deprecating and barren  skepticism  do not let yourselves be discouraged by  the sadness of certain  hours which pass over nations  live in the serene peace of laboratories  and libraries  say to yourselves first what have i done  for my instruction  and as you gradually advance what have i  done for my country  until the time comes when you may have  the immense happiness of thinking  that you have contributed in some way to  the progress and good  of humanity  [Music]  [Music]  you

